{
  "description": "Qwen3 training peaks on an RTX 5070 Laptop (8 GB), with the length of the longest training row of each run. They show what `max_length x batch` over-prices when the data is short, and how the estimate priced at the data's own length compares with the peaks. tests/test_vram_estimate_realised_length.py prices each run through profiler.profile_from_config. `rows` and `downstream_added_rows` were measured by a downstream application with its own runner; `bench_rows` were measured by the maintainers with `soup bench train` (torch's allocated and reserved peaks) beside a 200 ms whole-card nvidia-smi sampler. Every 4-bit row ran before #2609 was fixed (peft's fp32 embeddings and head kept on a bf16 card), so the tests price those rows with kbit_fp32.",
  "hardware": "NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 Laptop GPU, 8151 MiB, Windows 11 Pro",
  "stack": {
    "torch": "2.14.0+cu130",
    "downstream_soup_cli": "0.73.3 (a downstream build)",
    "bench_soup_cli": "origin/main 945a385dc"
  },
  "peak_kinds": {
    "torch_reserved_mib": "torch.cuda.max_memory_reserved() at the end of the downstream application's training run, MiB. It includes the caching allocator's slack and excludes the CUDA context. Reserved peaks of different runners are not comparable to better than about 10%: one recipe and data file gave 3388 MiB in the downstream runner and 3732 MiB in `soup bench train` (M3).",
    "max_memory_allocated_bytes": "torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated() from `soup bench train` (bench_rows only): the quantity the estimate's coefficients were calibrated on.",
    "device_mib": "whole-card nvidia-smi memory.used, maximum over the run's window, MiB. Sampled every 3-5 s in the downstream rows (a lower bound) and every 200 ms in bench_rows. device_clean is true only when nothing else was on the card (baseline 0 MiB before the run)."
  },
